      en: "The MacLehose Trail is a 100-kilometer hiking trail in Hong Kong, inaugurated in 1979 and named after former governor Sir Murray MacLehose. It comprises ten sections traversing the New Territories, offering diverse landscapes including beaches, woodlands, and mountain peaks. The highest point is Tai Mo Shan at 957 meters. The trail starts at Pak Tam Chung and ends at Tuen Mun. It is well-known for its clear waymarkers and distance posts. Hikers use it for day hikes or multi-day treks. The trail features geological diversity with volcanic rocks and granite formations. Facilities include campsites and shelters. The trail is maintained by the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department.",
